<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Fixed-width output with stdlib.fmt</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="ltlml.css"></head><body>
<nav class="ltlml-nav"><span class="nav-brand">LATERALUS</span>
<a href="./index.ltlml">Home</a><a href="./cookbook.ltlml">Cookbook</a></nav>
<main>
<h1>Fixed-width output with stdlib.fmt</h1>
<p>Tables read faster than prose, and a receipt is the smallest honest example of one. Two functions do the whole job: <code>pad_left</code> right-aligns, <code>pad_right</code> left-aligns.</p>
<h2>The two functions</h2>
<pre><code>fmt.pad_left("42", 5, "0")   // "00042"</code></pre>
<pre><code>fmt.pad_right("hi", 10, ".") // "hi........"</code></pre>
<p>Both take the width you want and the fill you want. The fill is a string, so a rule of dots is one call, not a loop of your own.</p>
<h2>Building the line</h2>
<pre><code>fn line(qty: int, item: str, cents: int) -> str {
    let q = fmt.pad_left(str(qty), 3, " ")
    let name = fmt.pad_right(item, 12, ".")
    let price = fmt.pad_left(str(cents), 6, " ")
    return q + "  " + name + " " + price
}</code></pre>
<p>Each column is padded to a fixed width before concatenation, so the separators never move. Quantities and prices are strings by the time they are padded - <code>str()</code> first, then align.</p>
<h2>What to watch</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Padding never truncates.</strong> A 15-character item in a 12-wide column pushes the price right. Either shorten the input or widen the column; the formatter will not decide for you.</li>
<li><strong>Pad before you concatenate,</strong> never after. Padding the finished line does nothing useful.</li>
<li><strong>Use spaces for numbers, dots for text.</strong> A dot rule between number groups reads as noise.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Expected output</h2>
<pre><code>  2  washers.....    149
 11  bolts.......     75
  1  torque wrench   4999</code></pre>
<p>The third row overflows the 12-wide column on purpose - it is the truncation caveat made visible.</p>
<p>Example: <code>examples/stdlib_fmt_inventory.ltl</code>. Vector: <code>examples/stdlib_fmt_inventory.expected</code>.</p>
</main></body></html>

